SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING
Issue 314 | Wednesday, March 18, 2026

Good morning.

1) The Fed decides today; equipment lenders are already moving
Two of the largest trailer lessors raised their rate cards last week in anticipation. Fleets that deferred equipment purchases in 2025 are now facing higher financing and higher tariffs on the equipment itself. Trailer orders fell to 9,000 units in February, the lowest since 2010.

2) The Texas cracker is back at 60% of capacity
Two weeks earlier than the operator's own guidance. Polyethylene spot gave back 3 cents. Converters expect allocation to end in April. The surcharge letters, however, have already gone out and will take longer to come back.

3) A regional grocery distributor's ERP outage stops shipments for two days
A failed upgrade over the weekend left the distributor's 40 stores without automated replenishment from Sunday night to Tuesday afternoon. Store managers ordered by phone. The distributor's statement blamed "a third-party integration"; the integrator's statement blamed the upgrade. Shelves were mostly fine, which says something about how much buffer stock these systems quietly carry.

NUMBER OF THE DAY: 11. Consecutive months of year-on-year decline in US Class 8 truck production. Suppliers are furloughing.

That's the morning. Reply with tips.

